# Version bump after commit

After creating a git commit in this repo (feature, fix, refactor, docs, etc.):

1. Increment the root `package.json` version patch of the pre-release: `0.1.0-alpha.N` → `0.1.0-alpha.(N+1)`.
2. Align `version` in any workspace `package.json` files changed in that commit (or newly added packages) to the same value.
3. For authored files touched in that work, set `@version` (and `@modified`) to the new version when those headers are maintained.
4. Commit the bump as a separate chore, e.g. `🏷️ Version: Bump to 0.1.0-alpha.N` (or include the bump in the same commit only if that is already the repo’s established pattern for that change). Prefer a dedicated follow-up commit when the feature commit already landed without a bump.
5. Do not skip the bump after a successful commit unless the user explicitly says not to version yet.
